&lt;h1&gt;


  Risk Management: Security Controls, Compliance, and Business Continuity
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity is not simply about preventing hackers from accessing computer systems. Organizations must also understand the risks that threaten their operations, sensitive information, financial resources, and reputation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management provides a structured approach to identifying potential threats, evaluating their impact, and implementing controls to reduce exposure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An effective cybersecurity risk management program helps organizations make informed security decisions, prioritize investments, and maintain business operations even when incidents occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What Is Cybersecurity Risk Management?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity risk management is the process of identifying, assessing, treating, and monitoring risks associated with information systems and organizational assets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A risk generally involves the possibility that a threat will exploit a vulnerability and cause harm to an organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may operate an internet-facing server that contains an unpatched vulnerability. A threat actor could exploit that weakness to gain unauthorized access, potentially exposing sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management helps organizations understand these scenarios and determine what actions should be taken to reduce their likelihood or impact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The primary objectives include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identifying critical assets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understanding potential threats and vulnerabilities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluating likelihood and business impact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Selecting appropriate security controls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Monitoring changes in risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Maintaining acceptable levels of organizational risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management should be an ongoing process because technology, threats, business operations, and regulatory requirements continuously change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Understanding Risk Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several concepts are important when evaluating cybersecurity risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Asset:&lt;/strong&gt; Something valuable to an organization, such as customer information, servers, applications, or intellectual property.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Threat:&lt;/strong&gt; Something capable of causing harm, such as a cybercriminal, malicious insider, natural disaster, or malware infection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability:&lt;/strong&gt; A weakness that could be exploited by a threat.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exploit:&lt;/strong&gt; A method or technique used to take advantage of a vulnerability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; The potential damage resulting from a successful attack or other adverse event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Likelihood:&lt;/strong&gt; The probability that a particular event will occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, consider a database containing sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The database is an asset. An external attacker is a potential threat. An unpatched vulnerability is a weakness. Exploiting that vulnerability could result in unauthorized access and a data breach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these relationships helps security teams evaluate which risks require the most attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Risk Assessment
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk assessment is the process of identifying and evaluating potential risks to an organization's systems and operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations commonly use qualitative and quantitative assessment methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Qualitative Risk Assessment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Qualitative assessments use descriptive categories to evaluate risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization might classify risks as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Low.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Medium.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;High.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Critical.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These classifications are based on factors such as likelihood, potential impact, and existing security controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A risk matrix can help visualize the relationship between likelihood and impact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a highly likely event with a severe business impact would generally receive a high-risk classification.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Qualitative assessments are useful when exact financial estimates are unavailable or when organizations need a relatively straightforward prioritization method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Quantitative Risk Assessment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quantitative risk assessment uses numerical values to estimate potential financial losses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One commonly used approach involves the following calculations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Single Loss Expectancy (SLE):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SLE = Asset Value × Exposure Factor&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exposure factor represents the percentage of an asset's value that could be lost during a particular incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Annualized Rate of Occurrence (ARO):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ARO estimates how frequently a particular event is expected to occur each year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ALE = SLE × ARO&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, assume a business application has an estimated value of $100,000. A successful incident could result in a 40% loss of that value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The SLE would be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$100,000 × 0.40 = $40,000&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the organization estimates that the event could occur twice per year, the ARO would be 2.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ALE would therefore be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$40,000 × 2 = $80,000&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This calculation estimates an annual expected loss of $80,000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quantitative methods can help organizations compare security investments with potential financial losses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, these estimates depend on assumptions and should not be treated as exact predictions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Risk Treatment Strategies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After identifying and evaluating risks, organizations must decide how to handle them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four commonly recognized risk treatment strategies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Risk Mitigation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk mitigation involves implementing controls to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may deploy multifactor authentication, patch vulnerable systems, encrypt sensitive information, and improve network segmentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These controls do not necessarily eliminate risk, but they can reduce it to an acceptable level.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Risk Acceptance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk acceptance occurs when an organization knowingly decides to tolerate a particular risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This may happen when the potential impact is relatively low or when the cost of implementing additional controls exceeds the expected benefit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accepted risks should be documented, reviewed, and approved by the appropriate stakeholders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Risk Transfer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk transfer involves shifting some financial consequences of a risk to another party.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity insurance is a common example.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An organization may purchase insurance to help cover certain costs associated with a data breach or cyberattack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, insurance does not eliminate the underlying security risk, and coverage depends on the policy's terms and exclusions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Risk Avoidance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk avoidance involves eliminating an activity that creates unacceptable risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may decide not to deploy an application that cannot meet its security requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoidance is appropriate when the potential risk is too significant to justify continuing the activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Security Controls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security controls are safeguards designed to protect organizational assets and reduce cybersecurity risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Controls can be classified by their purpose and how they operate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Control Categories by Function
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Preventive controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Attempt to stop incidents before they occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include firewalls, access controls, encryption, and multifactor authentication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Detective controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Identify suspicious activity or security violations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include intrusion detection systems, security monitoring, and audit logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Corrective controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Help restore systems or correct security weaknesses after an incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include patching, malware removal, and restoring systems from backups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deterrent controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Discourage individuals from attempting unauthorized activities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include warning banners, security policies, and visible surveillance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Compensating controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Provide an alternative safeguard when the preferred control cannot be implemented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if an older application cannot support modern authentication, an organization may isolate it behind a secure gateway and restrict access to approved systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Control Categories by Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security controls can also be categorized based on how they are implemented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Technical controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Firewalls, encryption, antivirus, and access management systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Administrative controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Security policies, procedures, risk assessments, and employee training.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Physical controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Locks, security guards, cameras, and access badges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A mature security program uses multiple control types to provide defense in depth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Compliance and Security Frameworks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations often need to meet regulatory requirements, contractual obligations, and internal security standards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compliance helps ensure that appropriate safeguards are implemented and that organizations can demonstrate adherence to applicable requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several widely used frameworks and standards support cybersecurity risk management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF):&lt;/strong&gt; Provides guidance for managing cybersecurity risks through the functions Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ISO/IEC 27001:&lt;/strong&gt; Defines requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an Information Security Management System.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PCI DSS:&lt;/strong&gt; Establishes security requirements for organizations that store, process, or transmit payment card information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HIPAA:&lt;/strong&gt; Establishes requirements for protecting certain health information handled by covered entities and business associates in the United States.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SOC 2:&lt;/strong&gt; Provides an independent examination and reporting framework concerning controls relevant to specified trust services criteria.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations should understand which requirements apply to their operations rather than assuming that every framework is mandatory for every business.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compliance is also not the same as complete security. An organization may satisfy a particular standard and still have vulnerabilities that require attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management must also consider events that interrupt normal business operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Business Continuity Planning (BCP) focuses on maintaining essential business functions during and after disruptive events.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disaster Recovery (DR) focuses more specifically on restoring IT systems, applications, and data following a disruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if ransomware makes an organization's production systems unavailable, its business continuity plan may identify alternative processes for maintaining critical operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Its disaster recovery plan may define how to restore servers, applications, and data from verified backups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two important recovery metrics are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recovery Time Objective (RTO):&lt;/strong&gt; The maximum targeted amount of time a system or business process can remain unavailable before recovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recovery Point Objective (RPO):&lt;/strong&gt; The maximum targeted amount of data loss measured in time that an organization can tolerate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an RTO of four hours means the organization aims to restore the affected service within four hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An RPO of one hour means the organization aims to limit data loss to no more than approximately one hour of updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These objectives help organizations select appropriate backup schedules, recovery technologies, and infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Third-Party and Supply Chain Risk
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations frequently rely on external vendors, cloud providers, software developers, and service providers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These relationships can introduce cybersecurity risks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a company may have strong internal security controls but depend on a third-party provider that stores sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If that provider experiences a data breach, the company may still face operational, financial, legal, and reputational consequences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Third-party risk management involves evaluating vendors before establishing relationships and monitoring their security practices over time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Important activities include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reviewing vendor security policies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluating independent security assessments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Assessing data handling and access requirements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reviewing incident notification procedures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Including security requirements in contracts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Monitoring&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hari Kamis, dua jam sebelum rilis, dan Dari satu-satunya orang yang tahu langkah cutover mana saja yang sudah selesai. Checklist-nya ada di satu tab spreadsheet, runbook-nya di shared drive, dan diskusinya di thread chat yang tidak bisa ditemukan siapa pun. Yang sebenarnya dibutuhkan tim adalah aplikasi mungil dengan empat checkbox dan dua penanggung jawab, tepat di sebelah file-filenya. Menulisnya gampang. Meng-host-nya, memberinya database, menyimpan key-nya dengan aman, dan memberinya URL, bagian itulah yang biasanya macet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Apa itu kategori Custom App
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;osFoundry&lt;/a&gt; mengumpulkan semua yang dibutuhkan untuk membuat dan menjalankan aplikasi kecil buatan sendiri dalam satu kategori di sidebar aplikasi desktop, bernama CUSTOM APP. Isinya lima layar. Room adalah ruang bersama tempat aplikasi tinggal sebagai tab. App Code adalah repository di balik sebuah aplikasi. Deployed Apps adalah daftar, untuk seluruh workspace, berisi apa saja yang sedang live. Snippets mengumpulkan aplikasi mungil yang tidak pernah butuh repository. Domains mengelola alamat web tempat aplikasi-aplikasi itu disajikan. Kalau kamu kenal Notion, bayangkan sebuah halaman yang tab-tabnya berisi aplikasi yang berjalan, bukan dokumen, dengan lapisan hosting kecil ala Vercel di bawahnya: setiap aplikasi punya database, secret, scheduled job, dan subdomain sendiri, seperti dijelaskan di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/features/internal-apps" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;halaman fitur aplikasi internal&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sekilas layar build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgn8mxrevrdmyt1lec0nf.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgn8mxrevrdmyt1lec0nf.jpg" alt="Tampilan build di aplikasi desktop osFoundry dengan daftar Pages, preview kosong, kotak deskripsi dengan tombol Build my site, dan panel chat Maestro" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cara tercepat melihat bagaimana sebuah aplikasi dimulai adalah lewat tampilan build di aplikasi desktop. Tangkapan layar ini dari builder Websites (contoh proyek "Hearth Bakery"), yang memakai alur deskripsikan-lalu-build yang sama dengan yang ditawarkan Room lewat kotak "Describe the app you want".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pages, di kiri: bagian aplikasi yang sudah ada, dengan Add page di bawahnya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preview, di tengah: apa yang sedang kamu bangun, dengan ukuran desktop, tablet, dan ponsel di atasnya. Label "On this computer" menandakan belum ada yang dipublikasikan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kotak deskripsi: kamu tulis apa yang dilakukan aplikasinya, untuk siapa, dan kesan seperti apa yang diinginkan, lalu tekan Build my site. Maestro, asisten AI aplikasi ini, yang menulis file-filenya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Panel Maestro, di kanan: percakapan yang membangun dan mengubah aplikasi. Use a folder mengarahkannya ke materimu sendiri, bukan membiarkannya menebak-nebak.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Publish needs credits, di atas: build dan preview berjalan di komputermu; hosting adalah langkah yang memakai kredit workspace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Di dalam Room, idenya sama dengan susunan yang berbeda: deretan tab aplikasi di atas, aplikasi yang dipilih di tengah, dan panel kanan dengan dua tab, Maestro dan Details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use case 1: tim rilis menaruh checklist di tempat pekerjaannya berada
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seorang release manager dan tiga rekan timnya yang sedang menyiapkan peluncuran.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: langkah cutover, runbook, dan release tracker tersebar di tiga tool, dan di hari rilis tidak ada yang percaya pada spreadsheet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang mereka lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuat room bernama "Launch Ops" lewat New Room, lalu menambahkan release tracker dan file-file peluncuran sebagai tab.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Menekan tombol + di deretan tab. Pemilih New Tab menampilkan aplikasi yang terpasang dan sebuah katalog (Incident log, Retro board, On-call rota, Changelog builder), yang bisa difilter dengan All, Forked, atau Snippets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Di "Describe the app you want", klik Build a snippet dan minta checklist cutover dengan satu penanggung jawab per item. Snippet adalah mini-app yang bisa dijalankan dan terdiri dari beberapa file. Snippet tidak punya repository, versi, maupun deploy, dan langsung berjalan di dalam tab.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tetap mengobrol dengan Maestro di panel kanan selama checklist terbuka, dan memakai tab Details untuk melihat room ini tentang apa dan apa saja yang sudah terjadi di dalamnya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: satu room tempat checklist (di demo: Freeze the 2.4 branch, Run the order-index migration, Smoke-test the order desk, Post the release note) berada di sebelah runbook dan tracker. Nantinya, layar Snippets menampilkan semua snippet dari semua room. Dari sana seseorang bisa meng-Export satu snippet sebagai JSON untuk workspace lain, atau mem-Promote-nya menjadi repository sungguhan ketika sudah terlalu besar untuk sebuah tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use case 2: developer memberi tool internal backend yang sungguhan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seorang developer solo yang membuat tool pendaftaran dan pesanan untuk tim support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: versi pertama berjalan di laptop dengan file SQLite. Sekarang tiga tim membutuhkannya, tool ini harus memanggil API pembayaran, dan pembersihan setiap malam harus jalan tanpa ada yang perlu ingat menekan tombol.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4wy18hiqj2jz9u8ta06y.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4wy18hiqj2jz9u8ta06y.jpg" alt="Tab Database di IDE osFoundry yang menampilkan data/app.db dengan tabel migrations, sessions, dan users, serta baris-baris users dalam grid read-only" width="800" height="310"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang dia lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Selagi aplikasi masih berjalan lokal, buka proyeknya di IDE dan klik tab Database. Tab ini menemukan file &lt;code&gt;.db&lt;/code&gt; dan &lt;code&gt;.sqlite&lt;/code&gt; milik aplikasi dan membuka tabel dalam mode read-only, 200 baris per halaman (contoh data di atas), supaya kamu bisa melihat apa yang benar-benar disimpan aplikasi sebelum memindahkan apa pun.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memindahkan aplikasi ke room bersama. Dialog Move to cloud menyalin aplikasi ke Cloud room supaya semua anggotanya bisa memakainya, dan melaporkan tabel mana yang disalin dan mana yang tetap di perangkat. Aplikasi dan datanya juga tetap ada di perangkat ini.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuka pengaturan tab aplikasi. Di bagian Secrets, App Runtime Secrets menampilkan key yang dideklarasikan aplikasi; Add a custom secret menyimpan yang baru, dan Rotate mengganti sebuah nilai. Aplikasi lalu menampilkan "Secrets updated. Redeploy to apply changes."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mendeklarasikan pembersihan itu di bawah &lt;code&gt;container.cron&lt;/code&gt; dalam manifest aplikasi, lalu membuka Scheduled jobs dan klik Sync from manifest. Setiap job bisa di-pause, dilanjutkan, atau dijalankan sekali dengan Run now.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: aplikasi dengan database Postgres sendiri. Di osFoundry setiap Room App mendapat satu, dengan pgvector dan TimescaleDB terpasang, dan connection string-nya datang sebagai secret saat runtime (&lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/features/per-app-postgres" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Postgres per aplikasi&lt;/a&gt;). Database browser-nya punya editor baris dengan filter dan tampilan SQL khusus SELECT, dan Database snapshots menyediakan New snapshot, Restore, serta link download. Perubahan kode dilakukan di App Code, tempat developer bisa mengerjakan versi seperti version/2.4 tanpa menyentuh main, mengecek Manifest (entry point, visibilitas, connector yang dibutuhkan), dan mengikuti pohon Versions sebelum rilis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use case 3: pemilik toko memublikasikan halaman dan terus memantaunya
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seseorang yang menjalankan toko roti kecil dan tidak menulis kode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: dia ingin situs sederhana berisi menu dan jam buka, di alamatnya sendiri, dan ingin tahu berapa biaya agar situs itu tetap online.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6z1x0j0blwfzw0or1bkc.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6z1x0j0blwfzw0or1bkc.jpg" alt="Builder Websites di osFoundry yang menampilkan situs toko roti tiga halaman versi pertama di preview, dan balasan Maestro bahwa teksnya masih tebakan sampai pemilik membagikan menu dan foto" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang dia lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuka Websites, menekan New Website, mendeskripsikan toko roti, lalu menekan Build my site. Versi pertama (di atas, contoh) memakai teks placeholder, dan Maestro mengatakannya dengan terus terang.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Menekan Use a folder dan memilih folder berisi menu asli dan foto, lalu meminta perubahan dengan bahasa biasa, misalnya "Make the headline bigger."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kalau sudah pas, publikasikan. Hosting memakai kredit workspace. Who can open it menawarkan Workspace members only, Anyone with the link, atau login milik aplikasi itu sendiri; situs toko roti publik memakai Anyone with the link.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Di Domains, mendaftarkan domain atau menambahkan domain yang sudah dimiliki, lalu mengedit record DNS-nya. Events menunjukkan siapa mengubah record yang mana, dan Settings berisi perpanjangan, privasi, dan kode transfer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93mdy08e44jtz1itp23e.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93mdy08e44jtz1itp23e.jpg" alt="Contoh situs toko roti yang sudah jadi di preview osFoundry, dengan headline besar di atas foto roti dan Maestro yang mengonfirmasi perubahan headline" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: situs yang memakai teks dan fotonya sendiri, disajikan di domainnya sendiri. Deployed Apps kemudian menjawab pertanyaan yang benar-benar ditanyakan seorang pemilik. Apps menampilkan setiap aplikasi dan di mana ia disajikan, Activity menampilkan apa yang berubah, Usage menampilkan biaya build dan storage per aplikasi, dan Previews menampilkan versi preview yang masih berjalan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bagaimana ia terhubung dengan bagian lain osFoundry
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Custom App jarang bekerja sendirian. Maestro adalah asisten di panel kanan setiap Room dan di tampilan build; ia menulis dan mengedit kode bersamamu. IDE menyediakan tab Database, source control, dan editor untuk aplikasi yang berawal dari folder di komputermu. Websites adalah jalur yang lebih cepat kalau "aplikasi"-nya sebenarnya situs publik. Snippets hanya terhubung ke App Code lewat Promote, jadi tool sekali pakai bisa menjadi repository yang dirawat tanpa perlu ditulis ulang. Pekerjaan terjadwal di dalam aplikasi ditangani oleh cron job aplikasi, yang dijelaskan di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/platform/app-cron" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;halaman platform app cron&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pertanyaan yang sering diajukan pengguna baru
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Apakah Room itu ruang chat?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Bukan. Inti sebuah Room adalah tab-tab aplikasinya. Chat Maestro ada di panel pendamping di sebelah kanan, di samping Details room tersebut.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kapan sebaiknya membuat snippet, bukan aplikasi?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Ketika tool-nya hanya beberapa file dan cukup berjalan di dalam satu room, misalnya formatter tanggal atau pengecekan webhook. Menyimpan snippet tidak memasang apa pun dan tidak men-deploy apa pun. Kalau nanti butuh versi, secret, atau database, Promote snippet itu menjadi repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bisakah dua aplikasi berbagi satu database?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Setiap aplikasi punya Postgres sendiri. Berbagi dilakukan lewat izin eksplisit: tab Database di room menampilkan Incoming Requests, Active Grants, dan Outgoing Access, dan setiap permintaan bisa disetujui atau ditolak.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Apakah mencobanya berbayar?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Build dan preview di komputermu tidak butuh kredit. Publikasi, deploy, dan pendaftaran domain memakai kredit workspace. Kamu bisa mengunduh aplikasi desktopnya di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/download" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;osfoundry.io/id/download&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sell a Minecraft plugin, a FiveM script, a Discord bot or a desktop app, and someone will use it without paying.&lt;/strong&gt; Usually it isn't a skilled cracker. It's one buyer sharing their copy with five friends, or a jar reposted on a leak forum the week you launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't make piracy impossible, and trying to usually hurts paying customers more than pirates. What you can do is make sharing inconvenient, make leaks traceable, and make paying the easy option. This is the plan I'd follow, in order. Each step is worth doing on its own, so stop wherever the effort stops paying off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 0: Decide what you're actually fighting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Threat&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How common&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What stops it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A buyer shares their copy or key&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Very common&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-buyer keys and machine limits (steps 1-2)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A jar gets reposted on a leak site&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Common&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-buyer keys, watermarks, takedowns (steps 1, 6, 8)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Someone deletes your license check&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Less common, easier with AI editors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Core unlock and obfuscation (steps 4-5)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A determined cracker rewrites your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rare&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nothing fully. Make it slow and not worth it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most losses sit in the first two rows, and those are the cheapest to fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Give every buyer their own key, automatically
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single shared password or license file can't tell anyone apart. Give each purchase its own key, so you can see who's using what and switch off exactly one person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Don't do it by hand. Connect your store so keys are created at checkout. On BuiltByBit, an &lt;em&gt;External license key&lt;/em&gt; placeholder writes the buyer's key into the jar they download. Voxel Shop and SpigotMC have their own placeholders, and Tebex, Stripe, Gumroad, Lemon Squeezy and others can create a key through a webhook. Wire refunds in too, so a refunded purchase loses its key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Limit where a key can run
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A key that works everywhere is a key that gets shared. Set a limit that fits the product:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Plugins:&lt;/strong&gt; servers per key (1-3 is typical).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Desktop apps:&lt;/strong&gt; machines per key (2-3 covers a laptop and a desktop).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bots or web tools:&lt;/strong&gt; an IP address limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the limit is reached, a new machine is refused with a clear message, and the buyer can free a slot themselves. Be generous: a support ticket costs you more than one extra machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F240tm7fmqwk5ys1x4o29.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F240tm7fmqwk5ys1x4o29.png" alt="A policy with Max machines and Max IPs per key" width="800" height="590"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Check at startup, and never take a customer down
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check the license when the app starts, then again every few hours. No need to phone home every minute. Above all, &lt;strong&gt;only stop on a clear "no"&lt;/strong&gt; (revoked, suspended, expired, too many machines). If your license server can't be reached, keep running. BuiltByBit's DRM guidance says the same, and plugins that disable themselves when a server is down risk being removed. For a Minecraft plugin, I walk through this check line by line in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-add-license-keys-to-a-minecraft-plugin-in-10-minutes-bd6"&gt;How to add license keys to a Minecraft plugin in 10 minutes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For customers without internet, ship a &lt;strong&gt;signed offline license&lt;/strong&gt;: a file signed with a private key (Ed25519, for example) that your app verifies with the public key, and that expires on a date you set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Make the check hard to delete
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A license check is one &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; statement. Anyone with a decompiler, or an AI editor, can delete it. Two things make that pointless:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Core unlock.&lt;/strong&gt; Encrypt a small, essential piece of your code (a core class, default rules, a lookup table) and only decrypt it after a valid license check. Delete the check, and that piece is simply missing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tamper reports.&lt;/strong&gt; Hash your own classes and report a modified copy with the next license check, so you know which license runs a patched build.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Obfuscate your release builds
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rename classes and methods, encrypt strings and scramble control flow, so your check and your core are hard to find and patch. ProGuard gives a safe rename; ProGuard followed by Skidfuscator adds control flow and string encryption. Only obfuscate the build you sell, test it before upload, and keep the mapping file so you can read customers' stack traces. I wrote a step-by-step guide: &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-obfuscate-a-minecraft-plugin-free-step-by-step-283m"&gt;How to obfuscate a Minecraft plugin&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 6: Watermark every download
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Put something in each buyer's copy that names them: their license key in the jar, an ID in the manifest, a mark in the archive. When the jar shows up on a leak site, you know whose it was without guessing. It also deters sharing: people think twice when their copy carries their name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 7: Watch for sharing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You won't spot a shared key by reading logs. Get alerts for the patterns that matter: one key on many machines at once, a key checking in from two countries an hour apart, a machine trying random keys, a revoked key still in use, a key pasted in your Discord. Then act on the real ones (a customer who travels isn't a pirate).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj1dgbjlk0i5skyjapu7i.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj1dgbjlk0i5skyjapu7i.png" alt="Sentinel watches license checks and flags shared and leaked keys" width="800" height="525"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 8: Respond fast when something leaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the buyer from the watermark or the key in the leaked file.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revoke that key. Copies stop at their next check; everyone else is untouched.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a takedown notice (DMCA) to the site hosting it. Most marketplaces also have a DMCA form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revoke refunded and charged-back purchases too, or they keep a free copy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 9: Make paying easier than pirating
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best anti-piracy feature is a product that's easy to buy. A fair price, a trial, fast updates, real support and a Discord. A pirate gets an old jar and no help. A customer gets the update that fixes their bug today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The whole plan on one page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmhfwa04htlnlsq7qnosj.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmhfwa04htlnlsq7qnosj.png" alt="The plan, layer by layer: keys, limits, a safe check, core unlock, obfuscation, watermarks, alerts, response" width="800" height="440"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

